  "schema_version": 2,
  "description": "A declared coverage corpus of movie-file archive fixtures, each posting at least 100 MiB of archive (150 MiB incompressible movies in 32 MiB volumes). Every set declares a class. The headline class is the common shape of a real post: a stored (-m0), multi-volume RAR of already-compressed media, one clean set per source-locked RARLAB writer era from 3.93 to 7.23, each posted clear, with encrypted headers and with data-only encryption, plus PAR2 repair in the shapes real posts carry. Every PAR2 shape in the headline class is one observed post, the one nearest the median size of its group in the PAR2 census of 2026-10-13 (546 posts, 476 with parsed PAR2), copied by payload size, slice size, recovery block count and article size. Four are a single bare media file, which is what 92% of the census is: 5.29 GB in 2000 slices of 2643160 bytes with 40 recovery blocks, 1.45 GB in 4 MiB slices with 35, 1.84 GB in 10000000-byte slices with 37, and 738 MB in 369024-byte slices with 192. The stored RAR form from the 4.20, 5.00 and 7.23 writers copies the median RAR post instead: thirteen 30 MiB volumes, 2000 slices of 195596 bytes and 167 recovery blocks, in the same three postings. The damage is whole articles the server refuses, which is how a real post degrades: the light severity loses the articles that damage about five slices and the heavy severity uses about nine tenths of the recovery blocks, or for the RAR sets one interior volume listed but never posted. The census measured what posters wrote, not what servers later lost, so the two severities bound a repair and are not measured loss rates. All PAR2 is written by par2cmdline-turbo, whatever tool wrote the post the shape was taken from, and its recovery files follow that tool's own sizing. The breadth class proves the clients handle shapes they meet less often: a four-movie stored set with RAR5 quick-open records, the official 7-Zip 7z container, a small-file-heavy stored Blu-ray-shaped topology posted in scattered NZB order, a twelve-file bare media post of 17.4 GB under one PAR2 set of 4480000-byte slices and 390 recovery blocks (also an observed post), and the recovery forms the census holds no post of, each borrowing a real PAR2 shape instead of an invented one: PAR2 over 7z and RAR recovery volumes at the median RAR post (thirteen 30 MiB volumes, and for PAR2 the same 195596-byte slices and 167 recovery blocks), and seven PAR3 lanes, every standalone set written by a published rarpar release with the FFT Reed-Solomon code (lost-article and withheld-volume repair over stored RAR5 in that same shape, clear and with encrypted headers, light and heavy lost-article repair over one bare media file in the 4 MiB shape, FFT repair of a withheld file plus in-place damage over the twelve-file shape, and PAR3 embedded by the par3cmdline reference in a stored zip of the median RAR payload size, the one lane rarpar does not write), and the scattered-article pair: one stored RAR5 set of 8 GiB in 256 MiB volumes, cut into one repair block per 750 KiB article and damaged by articles the server refuses, spread evenly over the whole post at 1% and 7%, repaired once with PAR2 and once with PAR3's FFT code from the same withheld articles and the same redundancy, the many-blocks, many-missing regime the FFT code exists for. One more stored RAR5 set of 26 GiB has more 750 KiB articles than PAR2's 32768-block limit allows, so any PAR2 set over it needs blocks longer than one article; the same 7% article loss is repaired there by PAR3's FFT code in two interleaved cohorts at 10% redundancy, while the generator proves no PAR2 set at that redundancy could repair it. It also covers the containers that are not RAR: GNU tar plain, gzipped and xz-filtered, a bare xz stream, Info-ZIP stored, deflated, spanned and ZipCrypto-encrypted, 7-Zip's own zip writer deflated and AES-256 encrypted, the zip64 parser path in three shapes (Info-ZIP with zip64 forced at ordinary size, 7-Zip with one member past 4 GiB, and an Info-ZIP archive written to a pipe so its members carry data descriptors), the 7z codecs (LZMA, LZMA2 solid and non-solid, PPMd, BZip2, and LZMA2 with encrypted data and with encrypted headers), a stored RAR set wrapping a .tar.xz the way a post that ships a tarball does, SFV checksum sidecars beside a stored RAR set and beside a raw media file, and one uuencoded raw media post. RAR compression is present only in the breadth class and only over a compressible payload: RAR 4.20 at -m3, at -m5 with the PPMd text model forced on, and solid, and RAR 7.23 at -m3 and at -m5 solid and non-solid. Already-compressed media is not recompressed in the wild, so no headline fixture compresses. Fixture counts are coverage choices; the corpus does not claim a population distribution, and it makes no claim about how often any of these shapes is posted.",
